<section class="section" id="alliance-architecture" data-rev9="alliance-evidence-delta">
<div class="container">

<div class="section-head">
  <div>
    <div class="kicker">14 · Alliance architecture</div>
    <h2>A strong relationship landscape — without turning interest into commitment</h2>
  </div>

  <p>
    Converging Waters already has warm conversations and credible access paths
    across local, scientific, cultural, restoration, education, tourism and
    conservation networks. The opportunity is real; formal roles and
    institutional commitments remain unconfirmed.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="status">Relationship momentum · exploratory by design</div>

  <h3>
    Interest can be shown without presenting a coalition that does not yet exist
  </h3>

  <p>
    The matrix below is the authoritative relationship map.
    It distinguishes personal access, organizational status, timing and the
    next useful conversation. Detailed biographies remain in the people section;
    later citizen-science, guardian, tourism and legal sections own the deeper
    domain design.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="focus-head">
  <div>
    <div class="kicker">Near-term logic</div>
    <h3>Focus first on functions that reduce uncertainty</h3>
  </div>

  <p>
    Priority here is not prestige or long-term importance.
    It is which conversation can materially improve readiness now.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="focus-grid">

  <article class="focus-card yellow">
    <h3>Territorial process</h3>
    <p>
      Improve listening, representation, intercultural process and the route
      into communities before participation or guardianship structures become too fixed.
    </p>
    <b>Near-term path</b>
    Centro Bartolomé de Las Casas (CBC) or an equivalent
    territorial/intercultural process partner.
  </article>

  <article class="focus-card blue">
    <h3>Independent scientific validation</h3>
    <p>
      Complement WGA and citizen science with a Peruvian scientific/laboratory
      pathway for QA/QC, methods and independent analysis.
    </p>
    <b>Near-term path</b>
    Universidad Nacional de San Antonio Abad del Cusco (UNSAAC)
    + qualified laboratories.
  </article>

  <article class="focus-card mint">
    <h3>Warm introductions, not recruitment</h3>
    <p>
      Use trusted relationships to share the work, learn and open doors.
      A warm introduction or personal relationship does not create a project role
      or an institutional commitment.
    </p>
    <b>Near-term path</b>
    Clarify mutual value first; deepen only where the fit becomes explicit.
  </article>

</div>

<div class="meeting-head">
  <div>
    <div class="kicker">September field window</div>
    <h3>Start with one informal Sacred Valley convergence gathering</h3>
  </div>

  <p>
    A shared welcome can let local people meet Simona and each other
    without turning the first days into a sequence of formal meetings.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="gathering">

  <h3>Possible welcome / convergence gathering — invite list to confirm</h3>

  <p>
    One informal evening at Felipe's home, a restaurant or another comfortable
    local setting. The purpose is relationship-building and mutual orientation,
    not commitments or presentations of finished roles.
  </p>

  <div class="invite-grid">

    <div class="invite">
      <b>Paul Gambin + Alejandra Orosco</b>
      <span>Maleza Casa Estudio · culture, photography and social/territorial perspective.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="invite">
      <b>Pavel Marmanillo</b>
      <span>Pachachaca / Reforesta Calca · restoration and local environmental network.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="invite">
      <b>Renzo Zeppilli</b>
      <span>Nature tourism, birding, biodiversity and citizen-science perspective.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="invite">
      <b>Carlos Garavito Herrera</b>
      <span>Residencia KAI · art, curation, territory and a possible artist-led river-awareness network.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="invite">
      <b>Joaquín Randall</b>
      <span>Warm access to the restoration ecosystem / Valle Sagrado Verde.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="invite">
      <b>Green Heroes representatives</b>
      <span>Public-school environmental education and possible citizen-science access.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="invite">
      <b>Duilio Vellutino</b>
      <span>Adventure tourism and river-use perspective, if useful and available.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="invite">
      <b>Others</b>
      <span>Invite where the relationship adds learning or connective value.</span>
    </div>

  </div>
</div>

<div class="meeting-head">
  <div>
    <div class="kicker">Targeted conversations after the gathering</div>
    <h3>Only the meetings that answer a readiness question</h3>
  </div>

  <p>
    These can be in person or remote.
    The gathering does not replace specialist conversations;
    it reduces the need for every relationship to become a formal appointment.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="meeting-strip">

  <div class="meeting">
    <span>Citizen science</span>
    <b>School / Green Heroes pathway</b>
    <p>
      Test participation, safeguarding, field practicality and
      how public-school access could work.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="meeting">
    <span>Science</span>
    <b>UNSAAC / qualified laboratory</b>
    <p>
      Test QA/QC, independent validation, methods,
      analytical capacity and collaboration fit.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="meeting">
    <span>Territory</span>
    <b>CBC or equivalent</b>
    <p>
      Test territorial process, intercultural safeguards and representation
      before governance assumptions harden.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="meeting">
    <span>Restoration</span>
    <b>Valle Sagrado Verde / local restoration actors</b>
    <p>
      Learn from existing community-facing restoration practice
      and test complementarity.
    </p>
  </div>

</div>

<div class="conditional">
  <b>Sequencing note:</b>
  Duilio may be especially valuable as a later expert reviewer of the
  River Economy / Adventure Tourism study.
  Riberas del Vilcanota belongs to a later coordination stage,
  when Converging Waters / Willkamayu has a clearer field scope
  and can approach possible complementarity from a collaboration-first posture.
</div>

